 "id": "2026-11-01-ai-training-opt-out-signal",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {"id": "hook", "text": "Four A.I. giants agreed on one switch to refuse A.I. training. Will it work?"},
  {"id": "f1", "text": "On October thirtieth, OpenAI, Google, Microsoft and Anthropic said they will honor a new signal in robots dot text, the file websites use to give crawlers rules. A publisher adds one line, and participating companies should not train on those pages."},
  {"id": "f2", "text": "The proposal is a draft at the Internet Engineering Task Force, written by Anthropic's Kevin Kelley. It is not an official standard. Anthropic says it has started. OpenAI plans to in the coming months. Microsoft says February first, twenty twenty-seven. Google's documentation says early twenty twenty-seven."},
  {"id": "f3", "text": "The limits are clear. The signal covers training only, not how content is used afterwards. It is voluntary, with no penalty and no independent check. So the real test is whether companies keep their word."},
  {"id": "cta", "text": "Will most A.I. companies honor it by next year? Yes or no?"}
 ],
 "sections": [
